3:description: Page word captions again after a canvas size change, keeping every word you corrected, and find out why a track was left as it was.
9:[Word captions](/captions/word-caption-styles/#word-caption-pages) are split into pages that fit the canvas they were made on, so a narrower canvas can leave them too wide. Re-lay Captions splits them into pages again for the new width, from the words they already hold.
25:Frame Job looks at every track named **Captions** or **Captions N** that holds only text clips. A track whose pages are all in one of the five [word styles](/captions/word-caption-styles/), with the same [looks](/captions/word-caption-looks/), is paged again from the words its pages hold. Nothing is transcribed again, so nothing is uploaded or billed.
31:Every correction you typed is kept, because the new pages are made from the words on the old ones.
33:Each page is paged again inside its own clip, and the new pages take the old track's place:
35:- A page you split, trimmed or moved keeps its words where they are.
36:- Pages are never merged across clips. A re-lay can split a page that's too wide for the new canvas, but a wider canvas won't join short pages together.
38:- The new pages go on a track with the same name, in the same place in the track stack, hidden and locked if the old one was.
40:The new pages are drawn fresh from the style, so any other change you made to a page goes. That includes a colour, an outline, a size, a position, or an unticked **UPPERCASE**.
48:> Caption track "Captions 2" is kept as placed: it holds a page with no word timings.
53:| it holds a page with no word timings | It's a [Subtitles](/captions/subtitles/) or imported track, or a page lost its timing because you [changed its number of words](/captions/edit-captions/). |
54:| it holds a page none of whose words starts inside it | A trim or split left a page with no word that begins on it. |
25:Any other graphic is kept as placed and rescaled from the old canvas, like a title. That includes a graphic with the same area as the canvas but another shape, such as 1080 × 1920 on a 1920 × 1080 canvas.
